        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the real part of each complex number: the a in a + b j, the horizontal coordinate when the number is drawn as a point.\n\nComplex numbers come out of np.fft.fft (each frequency's amount and phase together), np.fft.rfft's spectrum, and np.linalg.eig for a matrix that turns. np.real gets an ordinary array back out: the in-phase (cosine) part of a spectrum, or a signal from np.fft.ifft whose imaginary part is just rounding left over. Real numbers pass through unchanged.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.real\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA number, list or array, complex or not. Every input is converted and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nreal:\nThe real part of each number, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe spectrum of 0 1 0 0 - a single pulse one step in - is 1, -1j, -1 and 1j: four complex numbers of length 1, each a quarter turn further round. Their real parts are 1 0 -1 0. A metro re-sends the pulse twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.real, np.imag and np.angle split a complex array; abs gives each number's length (its magnitude). np.fft.fft and np.linalg.eig make complex arrays. atan2 and hypot are the same pair of ideas for a point given as x and y.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the real part of each complex number: the a in a + b j, the horizontal coordinate when the number is drawn as a point.\n\nComplex numbers come out of np.fft.fft (each frequency's amount and phase together), np.fft.rfft's spectrum, and np.linalg.eig for a matrix that turns. np.real gets an ordinary array back out: the in-phase (cosine) part of a spectrum, or a signal from np.fft.ifft whose imaginary part is just rounding left over. Real numbers pass through unchanged.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.real\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA number, list or array, complex or not. Every input is converted and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nreal:\nThe real part of each number, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe spectrum of 0 1 0 0 - a single pulse one step in - is 1, -1j, -1 and 1j: four complex numbers of length 1, each a quarter turn further round. Their real parts are 1 0 -1 0. A metro re-sends the pulse twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.real, np.imag and np.angle split a complex array; abs gives each number's length (its magnitude). np.fft.fft and np.linalg.eig make complex arrays. atan2 and hypot are the same pair of ideas for a point given as x and y."
        },
